summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up of chronic problems.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Carolyn Brown is a 49-year-old female who presents today for follow-up of her chronic problems. The patient reports her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little bit lately. She notes that it is mostly in her elbows. She has been sitting at a computer at her desk more often, so she is not sure if that has increased the flare-ups for that. She wants to talk to me about that today.

The patient reports she is right handed. She notes her right elbow bothers her more than the other, but primarily she is typing all day versus writing, so it flares up in both. She notes she has some numbing in her hands when she types for long periods of time. She shakes out her arms a little bit to relieve that sensation.

The patient reports she has had a history of gout. Her last episode was about 3 months ago. She had inflammation of her toe. She has not had any other issues with that. The medication I gave her has really controlled it, and she has not seen a flare since.

The patient reports her psoriasis has been under control. She has not had a major problem with it either.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or night sweats.
• Musculoskeletal: Reports joint pain in her elbows. Endorses right elbow pain more than left elbow. Endorses right elbow pain with palpation of olecranon, pain with pronation and supination, and pain with flexion and extension.
• Neurological: Reports numbness in her hands when typing for long periods of time.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Neck: No cervical lymphadenopathy.
• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
• Cardiovascular: Normal heart rate and rhythm.
• Musculoskeletal: Edema and inflammation of the right olecranon. Pain to palpation of the olecranon, pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm. Examination of the abdomen is normal. No lower extremity edema.

RESULTS

Autoimmune profile is unremarkable.
ESR is within normal limits.
Uric acid is within normal limits.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Ms. Brown is a 49-year-old female who presents today for follow-up of her chronic problems.

Gout.
• Medical Reasoning: Her last episode was about 3 months ago. She had inflammation of her toe. She has not had any other issues with that. The medication I gave her has really controlled it, and she has not seen a flare since.
• Medical Treatment: Continue colchicine 0.6 mg twice daily as needed for flare ups.

Osteoarthritis.
• Medical Reasoning: She reports that her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little bit lately. She notes it is mostly in her elbows. She has been sitting at a computer at her desk more often, so she is not sure if that has increased the flare ups for that.
• Medical Treatment: Order right elbow x-ray. Prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day.

Psoriasis.
• Medical Reasoning: She reports her psoriasis has been under control.
• Medical Treatment: Continue clobetasol for scalp.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
