summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Annual wellness exam.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Beverly Smith is a 24-year-old female who presents for her annual wellness exam. She has a history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension.

The patient reports that her blood sugars have been elevated. She attributes this to her diet, which has been poor lately. She has been taking her metformin twice a day as prescribed, but she has been snacking a lot at work. She has been working at a bakery for the past few months and finds herself eating a lot of desserts.

Ms. Smith denies any sleep disturbances or any concerns about self-harm. She reports that she gets a full eight hours of sleep per night. She denies any other symptoms.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies weight loss or gain.
• Eye: Denies vision changes.
• Ears, Nose, Mouth, and Throat: Denies ear pain, hearing loss, sinus pain, nasal congestion, or mouth lesions.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, palpitations, or shortness of breath.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath, wheezing, or cough.
• Neurological: Denies headache, weakness, or loss of balance.
• Psychiatric: Denies depression, anxiety, or suicidal ideation.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
• Cardiovascular: Grade 2 systolic ejection murmur. No carotid bruits. No thyromegaly. No edema.
• Musculoskeletal: No swelling in the legs. Equal bilateral upper extremity blood pressure. No focal weakness in the upper or lower extremities.

RESULTS

Hemoglobin A1c is elevated at 8.1.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Beverly Smith is a 24-year-old female who presents for her annual wellness exam. Her history of type 2 diabetes is out of control. Her hemoglobin A1c is elevated at 8.1. She is currently taking metformin twice a day. She is employed at a bakery and finds herself eating a lot of sweets. We discussed the importance of limiting her diet. She will check her blood sugars in the morning and follow up with me in 2 weeks. If her blood sugars are not under control, we will discuss adding another medication.

Medical Reasoning

The patient's elevated hemoglobin A1c is due to poor dietary control. She is currently taking metformin twice a day. I recommended that she limit her diet and check her blood sugars in the morning. We will follow up in 2 months to see if her blood sugars are under control.

Her hypertension is stable on lisinopril 10 mg once a day. There is no indication to make any changes at this time.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 2 months. }}<|eot_id|>
