Hypertension and palpitations.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male with a history of hypertension and palpitations. He was seen in the emergency room with high blood pressure and palpitations. He believes that his blood pressure was due to a panic attack as he had several cups of coffee and did not eat breakfast on that day. He denies any recurrence of palpitations. He states he has had issues with high blood pressure since he was young. He has a strong family history of hypertension. He is currently seeing a new primary doctor and was placed on Benicar HCT 40/25 about a month ago. He is also taking Amlodipine 10 mg daily. He states he checks his blood pressure at home every morning and it has been running 146-155. He is trying to reduce his salt intake. He reports that he is trying to walk on the treadmill when he can.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Cardiovascular: Denies palpitations.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or swelling.
• Neurological: Denies headaches or dizziness.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Constitutional: In no apparent distress.
• Cardiovascular: No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds. No bruits.
• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
• Musculoskeletal: No edema. No tenderness to palpation.

RESULTS

Potassium 4.0 (reference range 3.6-5.1).

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Resistant hypertension.
• Medical Reasoning: He is currently on three medications at maximum dose and his blood pressure is still elevated. He has a strong family history of hypertension. His potassium is normal, but we will need to monitor his sodium levels.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the risks of his current regimen not being effective and the potential side effects of spironolactone. He will need to monitor his electrolytes and sodium levels. I encouraged him to reduce his salt intake and increase his potassium. He was provided with information on the DASH diet.
• Medical Treatment: We will start him on spironolactone. I advised him that he will need to be seen in two weeks to check his blood pressure and electrolytes. If his blood pressure is not controlled on spironolactone, we will send him to the resistant hypertension clinic. They will look into secondary causes of his hypertension and have access to newer procedure-based technologies to lower his blood pressure without medication, such as renal denervation.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in two weeks to check his blood pressure and electrolytes. }}<|eot_id|>
