summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Abdominal pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Brittany Edwards is a pleasant 32-year-old female who was last seen in August 2019 with flares of abdominal pain, who was diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome that was treated with Bentyl. She had a previous prescription for Reglan that she received from the emergency room that she used as needed for nausea. She was instructed to start a low dose fiber supplement, such as Citrucel, daily, probiotics to help with gas and bloating, Bentyl up to four times daily for intestinal cramping, and stop Reglan and use Zofran as needed. Neuromodulators such as Elavil and Buspar were discussed but not started.

The patient reports that she has been going out to eat on Friday nights and Saturday nights and that she has had some spicy foods or spicy shrimp. She states that she is sensitive to spicy foods and alcohol.

The patient reports that she is having bowel movements between 2 to 3 times a day. She notes that her stools are formed. She reports that she has more diarrhea when she goes out. She states that it is usually the food, but sometimes the alcohol. She notes that she usually does not get diarrhea when she eats at Friday's restaurant, but she does eat pasta with chicken alfredo. She notes that she always drinks water with her meal. She states that she has been avoiding heavy foods.

The patient reports that she has been eating fried chicken and pasta today.

The patient reports that she has been drinking tequila and dark liquor. She notes that she usually drinks 2 to 3 drinks.

She denies any tenderness.

MEDICATIONS

Bentyl 20 mg as needed.
Zofran as needed.
Citrucel.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Gastrointestinal: Examination of the abdomen is soft, non-tender. No masses or organomegaly. Active bowel sounds.

ASSESSMENT

Irritable bowel syndrome.
• Functional abdominal pain.
• Diarrhea.
• Nausea.

PLAN

Irritable bowel syndrome.
• I encouraged her to try Citrucel. She can take it twice a day for regularity.
• I advised her to use Bentyl 20 mg as needed 30 to 45 minutes prior to eating out at a restaurant. She can also use Zofran if needed.
• I advised her to reduce greasy and fatty foods and alcohol consumption.
• I encouraged her to follow up in one month by email regarding any side effects and issues, or issues with Elavil or if adjustments are needed.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
