summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow up adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Christopher Watson is a 86-year-old male who returns in follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. The patient is accompanied by his daughter, Theresa Watson. The patient is being seen for the first time since undergoing shunt surgery on 08/01/2020.

The patient reports that his walking has improved since surgery. He is now able to walk to the dining room. He also states he is doing better mentally. He states he is not feeling lost when speaking with people anymore.

The patient denies any headaches or pain where the shunt is. He also denies any pain in his belly.

Ms. Watson interjects and states that her father was not able to walk to the dining room prior to surgery. She also states that his bladder control did improve after surgery but has leveled off and he now wears diapers. She denies any sores from wearing the diaper.

Ms. Watson also states that her father has been doing much better since surgery.

Ms. Watson interjects and states that her sister, Hannah, stated that she can now have a normal conversation with her father again.

The patient is doing well. He denies any issues.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Adult hydrocephalus.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Mr. Christopher Watson is married. He denies smoking tobacco.

CURRENT MEDICATIONS

• Medtronic Strata Shunt, programmed at a level 2.0.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Negative for fever or unintentional weight loss.
• Genitourinary: Positive for urinary incontinence and wearing a diaper. Negative for genital sores, decreased or painful output.
• Neurological: Positive for continued walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Negative for headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.
• Psychiatric: Negative for depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

• Blood pressure: 124/80.
• Heart rate: 64.
• Respirations: 18.
• Weight: 174 pounds.
• Pain: 0/10.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Constitutional: Normal
• Gait: Slightly slow and unsteady gait without an assistive device. Tinetti assessment tool: Gait score 6 to 8/12 and balance score 12/16 for a total score of 18 to 20/28. This is slightly improved from his initial score of 15 to 17/28.
• Neurological: Cranial nerves are intact. Pupils are equal. EOMs are intact. Face is symmetric. No dysmetria. Motor normal for bulk and strength. Coordination slow from no-finger to nose.

RESULTS

CT scan of the head 10/15/2020 reveals a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of Monro of 4.6 centimeters with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 millimeters. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right front region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Adult hydrocephalus.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well and has shown improvement since his shunt surgery. The CT scan shows a tiny amount of blood in the right parietal region, but this is not causing any symptoms.
• Medical Treatment: I have recommended that we wait another 2 to 3 months before we change the shunt.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I have discussed the risks and benefits of continuing to wait to change the shunt. The patient is satisfied with the plan.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient has shown improvement in his gait and cognitive abilities.
• Medical Treatment: I have recommended that we continue to monitor his progress.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I have discussed the importance of following up regularly.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient's urinary incontinence and urgency have not improved.
• Medical Treatment: He will continue to wear a diaper.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I have advised him to report any sores or pain associated with wearing the diaper.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
