summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Tyler Nelson is a 70-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the management of his rheumatoid arthritis. The patient last saw me about a month ago and received a steroid injection in his right knee. The patient reports that the injection did help for a couple of weeks. The patient reports that his right knee is still painful and that his entire leg is now swollen. The patient states that he has been wearing compression socks, but it has not helped. The patient reports that his leg swelling has kept him from doing things during the day. The patient states that he has slowed down a little bit and has found a few things too fast. The patient reports that when he has been sitting for a while, he has to try and get up and he is so stiff that he has to use a cane to get his bearings. He states that after a little bit, it loosens up. The patient states that he has been taking his Xeljanz 11 mg daily and Celebrex 200 mg daily as prescribed. He started taking the Celebrex about 2 months ago.

The patient reports that he has been having pain in his right knee for about 2 months now. He states that before his knee was swollen, but not the whole leg. The patient denies any weakness in his leg.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
The patient has a history of rheumatoid arthritis.

SURGICAL HISTORY

The patient had a right knee injection 1 month ago.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that he enjoys salty snacks.

CURRENT MEDICATIONS

Xeljanz 11 mg daily.
Celebrex 200 mg daily.

ASSESSMENT

Rheumatoid arthritis.
Right knee pain.
Bilateral lower extremity edema.

PLAN

I explained to the patient that we will give him another steroid injection for his right knee. I also want to get an x-ray of his right knee to see where all the swelling is coming from. I want to set up physical therapy for him as well. I also advised the patient to reduce his salt intake. I advised the patient to try and elevate his legs at night. I advised the patient to follow up with his PCP if nothing works.

PROCEDURE

Fluid aspiration and steroid injection.
The risks, benefits, and alternatives were explained. The risks of steroid injection were explained including, but not limited to: hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hypoglycemia among others. Verbal consent was attained. The right knee was accessed through the lateral approach with 3 mL of initially clear fluid, then bloody fluid. The right knee was prepped in a standard-fashion. The right knee was injected with 80 mg of triamcinolone, and 1 mL of lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well, with no complications. Post-procedure instructions were given. Patient will follow up with his PCP. }}<|eot_id|>
