summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Rash.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Jacqueline Miller is a pleasant 47-year-old female who presents for follow-up of rash. She is doing well. She reports that she has a small bit leftover. She started taking doxycycline only one a day because she thinks the pharmacist said if it's getting better, to just limit it to once a day. She was taking it twice a day and did notice a lot of improvement. She started doing only once a day and it seems the same and it's not continuing to get better. She denies breastfeeding at this time. She stopped breastfeeding and then asked for the doxycycline at that time.

MEDICATIONS

Doxycycline 100 mg tablet, twice a day, started 2 weeks ago.
Sulfacet wash, twice a day.
Metro cream, after face wash.

ALLERGIES

None.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Ears, Nose, Mouth, and Throat: Denies ear, nose, and throat pain or discomfort.
• Eyes: Endorses ocular rosacea.
• Skin: Endorses rash.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Skin: Fitzpatrick skin type IV, not prone to burning, tans easily. Bilateral medial cheeks few faint erythematous papules, little bit of redness around and underneath the nostrils.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Perioral dermatitis.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient is concerned about the rash. She has been taking doxycycline once a day for the past week. I recommend continuing to take the doxycycline twice a day.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained that the doxycycline can cause heartburn or stomach upset. I also explained that she should continue to use the face wash twice a day. I explained that the doxycycline can make her skin more sensitive to the sun, so she should use sunscreen.
- Medical Treatment: Continue doxycycline 100 mg tablet twice a day. Continue to use the face wash twice a day. Apply metro cream after washing the face.

2. Rosacea.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient endorses ocular rosacea.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained that rosacea can cause redness and flushing. I explained that she should continue to use the face wash twice a day. I explained that she should avoid using products that can cause irritation.
- Medical Treatment: Continue to use the face wash twice a day.

3. Dry skin.
- Medical Reasoning: She has been using the face wash twice a day. I explained that she should continue to use the face wash once a day and apply a moisturizer.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained that the doxycycline can cause dry skin. I explained that she should use a moisturizer.
- Medical Treatment: I gave her a sample of Vanicream. I also gave her a sample of petroleum jelly. She can use either of these for dry skin.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 4 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
