summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Abdominal pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Bobby Miller is a 76-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of abdominal pain. The patient was seen in the emergency room yesterday for similar complaints. The patient reports that his abdominal pain started a few weeks ago. He denies vomiting but notes that he has experienced some nausea and low-grade fevers. He also endorses some pain that radiates to his right side. The patient reports that the pain is severe and that he has been unable to eat since the pain started. The patient reports that he is a type 2 diabetic and has been managing his diet and exercise. He denies any family history of gallbladder disease.

The patient reports that he has been having pain in his right upper quadrant. He describes the pain as severe and rates it as an 8 out of 10. He notes that the pain is intermittent and can occur at any time, but is most often after meals. The patient also notes that he has been having nausea and vomiting.

The patient was seen in the emergency room yesterday for similar complaints. The patient underwent an ultrasound of his abdomen which revealed multiple gallstones.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.
• Gastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies back pain.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Respiratory: Afebrile, normal work of breathing.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats, rubs, or murmurs.
• Gastrointestinal: Soft, nontender abdomen. Positive bowel sounds. Slight guarding to palpation in the right upper quadrant. No rebound tenderness.

RESULTS

Abdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder is also noted. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with symptoms consistent with cholecystitis with gallstones. The patient has not had any improvement with dietary modifications, and therefore surgical intervention is recommended.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained to the patient that he will need to have a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. I advised him that this procedure will be performed in the operating room and will take about an hour to an hour and a half. He will need to spend the night in the hospital post procedure for observation. I advised him that he will need to avoid high-fat foods postoperatively. He will need to follow up with his primary care physician to continue to manage his type 2 diabetes.
- Medical Treatment: We will proceed with laparoscopic cholecystectomy.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with his primary care physician to continue to manage his type 2 diabetes. The patient will also follow up with me in a week to ensure that he is healing well after the surgery. }}<|eot_id|>
