summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Jeremy Davis is a pleasant 66-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of back pain. The onset of his symptoms began 4 months ago when he was moving boxes for his son moving out of his college dorm. The patient reports pain when he is sitting for a very short period of time, or when he turns a certain way or rolls over in his sleep. He also reports a tingling sensation in his right foot that resolves after 1 hour. He also reports some difficulty with carrying heavy objects. He denies urinary or bowel incontinence. The patient reports that he has tried Tylenol, ibuprofen, heat, and ice without relief. He also denies any stomach upset from ibuprofen. He had an urgent care visit where he was recommended to get an MRI of his lumbar spine, but he was concerned because they had already maxed out their insurance. He is now concerned about his symptoms and wants to move forward with the MRI.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever or chills.
• Musculoskeletal: Reports lower back pain. Denies leg weakness.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological
- Orientation: Grossly intact.
- Cranial Nerves: Grossly intact.
- Motor: Strength is equal bilaterally.
- Reflexes: Positive straight leg raise.
- Sensation: No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine.

Vital Signs
- Temperature: Normal
- Blood Pressure: 117/56.
- Heart Rate: 68.
- Oxygenation: 95% on room air.

RESULTS

X-rays of the lumbar spine taken in office today reveal no acute bony abnormality. This is not surprising based on the history provided by the patient.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Acute disk herniation.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient reports a history of back pain, tingling in his right foot, and difficulty carrying heavy objects. He had an urgent care visit 4 months ago and was recommended to get an MRI of his lumbar spine, but he was concerned because they had already maxed out their insurance. He is now concerned about his symptoms and wants to move forward with the MRI.
- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of getting an MRI of his lumbar spine. I explained to him that x-rays do not show soft tissue and that an MRI would be able to show us what is going on in his lumbar spine.
- Medical Treatment: We will refer the patient to a physical therapist through our health system. We will also prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once daily to help with his pain.

Additional Treatment Options
- Medical Reasoning: If the patient's symptoms do not improve with physical therapy and medication, we may consider other treatment options such as an epidural steroid injection to help take down some of the inflammation.
- Patient Education and Counseling: We will discuss this option with the patient and answer any questions he may have.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
