Dennis Taylor is a 57-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of shortness of breath. He has a history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma. The patient states he has been experiencing shortness of breath for about a couple of weeks. He wakes up and goes to bed with shortness of breath. He also has been coughing up blood for the past 3 weeks. He has been coughing up 3 to 4 cups of blood daily. He denies hematemesis, melena, or hematuria. He also denies any leg swelling. The patient reports a recent history of knee pain. He denies any recent travel or recent hospital visits. He has been having trouble getting an appointment in the office due to being booked up.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Reports shortness of breath.
Respiratory: Reports shortness of breath and hemoptysis.
Cardiovascular: Denies any chest pain or palpitations.
Gastrointestinal: Denies hematemesis, melena, or hematuria.
Genitourinary: Denies hematuria.
Musculoskeletal: Reports knee pain.

