summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right knee injury.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Scott Smith is a pleasant 42-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The onset of his pain began 3 days ago while playing soccer. He states that he felt a pop in his right knee while trying to plant kick a ball. He denies any previous knee injuries. The patient states that he is a soccer player and has been playing soccer for a long time. He denies any other sports injuries.

The patient reports that he is still able to bear weight on the right knee but it feels loose. He states that he has been experiencing pain on the right knee. He rates his pain level as 6 out of 10. He states that he has been taking anti-inflammatories which has helped a little bit. He denies any numbness or tingling in his right leg.

The patient reports that he is unable to fully extend his right knee and that it hurts at the end of the range of motion. He also reports that he is unable to fully flex his right knee and that it feels loose. He denies any pain with palpation of the right knee.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports that he is married and works at a desk. He is an avid jogger.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain. Denies right leg numbness or tingling.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK: Examination of the right knee: Significant effusion. Slight ecchymosis. Pain with palpation to the medial joint line. Positive Lachman exam. Limited ROM, cannot fully extend or flex.

RESULTS

X-rays of the right knee taken in the office today are within normal limits.

ASSESSMENT

Right knee injury, suspected anterior cruciate ligament tear.

PLAN

I discussed the diagnosis and treatment options with the patient today. I have recommended an MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the anterior cruciate ligament. We will reevaluate once the results are back. I have also recommended that the patient limit his activities and wear a knee immobilizer when walking. I have also recommended the use of crutches and ice when needed.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me once he receives the results of his MRI. }}<|eot_id|>
