HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Gregory Smith is a pleasant 45-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of his symptoms began approximately 2.5 weeks ago when he twisted his right ankle stepping off a curb. He states that he did not fall down at the time of the injury, but he did experience a twisting injury to his right knee. The patient states that he felt a pop or a click in his right knee at the time of the injury. He denies any pain in his right knee at rest. He states that he experiences pain when he stands on one foot for an extended period of time. He also reports that he experiences clicking in his right knee when he does squats. The patient denies any numbness or tingling in his foot, but he does have a history of a right second toe fracture that has resulted in some numbness depending on the shoe he wears. The patient states that he has fractured his second toe 3 times over the last 10 years. The patient reports that he works in sales and is active in his free time. He reports that he is able to ride a bike, but has not been doing so for the winter. The patient states that he is not able to do squats. The patient denies any pain with palpation of the lateral aspect of his right knee. He states that he has taken ibuprofen and ice for his pain. He reports that he is not a big pill taker, so he has not taken much ibuprofen.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain. Positive varus test.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK: Examination of the right knee: Slight effusion. There is no pain to palpation of the right lateral knee. Positive varus test. Full range of motion. There is no pain with ligamentous testing. Negative Lachman's test.

RESULTS

