Elevated cholesterol.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Julie Cruz is a 17-year-old non-binary, immensipated minor who presents for evaluation of newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia. The patient reports that she was found to have elevated cholesterol levels on a finger prick test at her annual checkup and was referred to our clinic.

The patient reports that she is active and enjoys riding her bike. She also enjoys cooking and tries to eat a healthy diet.

The patient denies any family history of hypercholesterolemia.

The patient also reports that she identifies as non-binary and has not been in contact with her parents for a while.

The patient also reports that she has a history of sudden death in her family.

PAST HISTORY

Medical: The patient reports no significant past medical history.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports that she is active and enjoys riding her bike. She also enjoys cooking and tries to eat a healthy diet. The patient also reports that she is non-binary and has not been in contact with her parents for a while. The patient denies smoking. The patient also reports that she has a history of sudden death in her family.

MEDICATIONS

None.

VITALS

Blood pressure is within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.
Respiratory: No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
Eyes: No jaundice.
Neck: No thyroid enlargement.
Abdomen: No hepatomegaly or splenomegaly.

RESULTS

Cholesterol panel was elevated at 210.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Elevated cholesterol.

Patient presents for evaluation of newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia. The patient was found to have an elevated cholesterol level on a finger prick test at her annual checkup. She was referred to our clinic for further evaluation.

Medical Reasoning
I explained to the patient that the American Academy of Pediatrics recommends that all children be screened once they become between the ages of 9 and 11 and again between ages 17 and 20. I explained that this is because of the link between high cholesterol and premature heart attacks. I explained that we will be ordering a full cholesterol panel, as well as a complete metabolic panel, glucose, thyroid studies, and liver panels. I also explained that we will be performing genetic testing to see if this problem is familial or secondary.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

Patient Questions and Answers
I asked the patient if she has any questions. She denied having any questions.

INSTRUCTIONS

I instructed the patient to follow a well-balanced diet that includes a variety of foods that are lower in saturated fats and sugars. I also instructed her to meet with a nutritionist. She will continue to be active and exercise. She will follow up with another cholesterol level in 3 to 6 months after she has met with the nutritionist and instituted dietary changes.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

CONCLUSION

The patient is a 17-year-old non-binary, immensipated minor who presents for evaluation of newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia. I have explained the diagnosis and treatment options in a manner she can understand. She is currently following a healthy diet and staying active. I have recommended that we order a full cholesterol panel, as well as a complete metabolic panel, glucose, thyroid studies, and liver panels. I also explained that we will be performing genetic testing to see if this problem is familial or secondary. She will follow up with another cholesterol level in 3 to 6 months after she has met with a nutritionist and instituted dietary changes. }}<|eot_id|>
