summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Left knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Angela Brown is a pleasant 46-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of left knee pain. The onset of her pain began one week ago when she was running with her niece. She states that she heard a pop in her knee at the time of injury. She denies any trauma, but states that she twisted her knee. She reports that she has been limping since the injury and has not been able to bear weight on the left leg. She denies any trauma or injury to the contralateral knee. She rates her pain as 4 out of 10 in severity. She states that her pain has been worsening and is now a throbbing pain. She denies any locking or buckling. She reports that she has been taking Tylenol and wrapping her knee with kinesiology tape. She is unable to take ibuprofen per her physician due to a recent ulcer. She has also been doing some ice to her knee.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports left knee pain.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK: Examination of the left knee: Edema present. Effusion present. Moderate range of motion. Pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman's test. Negative valgus and varus test.

RESULTS

X-rays of the left knee were obtained and reviewed today. They are normal. There is no evidence of any fractures or bony abnormalities.

ASSESSMENT

Left knee pain.

MEDICAL REASONING

The patient is a 46-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of left knee pain. The onset of her pain began one week ago when she was running with her niece. She reports that she heard a pop in her knee at the time of injury. She denies any trauma, but states that she twisted her knee. She denies any trauma or injury to the contralateral knee. She rates her pain as 4 out of 10 in severity. She states that her pain has been worsening and is now a throbbing pain. She denies any locking or buckling. She reports that she has been taking Tylenol and wrapping her knee with kinesiology tape. She is unable to take ibuprofen per her physician due to a recent ulcer. She has also been doing some ice to her knee.

Based on her examination today, I believe that the patient has a medial collateral ligament strain. The x-rays of her left knee were normal and there is no evidence of any fractures or bony abnormalities.

PLAN

I have reviewed the patient's examination and x-rays in detail today. I have had a lengthy discussion with her regarding my assessment and plan. I have recommended that we start her on tramadol 50 mg twice a day. She can continue to take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. She will be placed in a knee immobilizer and will need to keep her leg still for the next week. She will also need to ice her knee three to four times a day for 20-30 minutes at a time. I also recommended that she avoid running for the next three weeks. I encouraged her to contact the office if she feels like her pain is getting worse. }}<|eot_id|>
